# AS_BUILT.md is an append-only log: every change adds an entry at the top, so
# two open PRs conflict by construction rather than by subject. Every resolution
# has been the same mechanical "keep both" — five rebases in one evening across
# four unrelated PRs at the worst of it, which is what motivated the (now
# reversed) one-file-per-entry split.
#
# THIS LINE USED TO SAY `merge=union` (#308), AND UNION IS NOT SAFE HERE.
# Union resolves a conflicting hunk by taking both sides' UNIQUE lines around the
# lines they SHARE. Two entries written by the same generator share plenty — a
# "What changed:" lead-in, a "Verified with a control." sign-off, a blank line —
# and the shared lines are emitted ONCE, at the end. Measured on git 2.50.1 with
# the exact attribute #308 shipped: two entries appended concurrently merged at
# exit 0, with the first entry's closing line absorbed into the second. No
# conflict, no marker, nothing to review, one line of history quietly relocated.
# Union also emits ours-then-theirs regardless of date, so it produced 2026-08-15
# above 2026-08-17 in a file whose first line promises newest-first.
#
# `as-built-log` is the entry-aware driver in scripts/git/as-built-log-merge.ts.
# Its unit is a WHOLE ENTRY (a `## ` heading and everything under it), so two
# entries are never spliced together and the result stays newest-first. What it
# will not merge — both sides editing one entry, both reordering differently, a
# side truncated to nothing — it REFUSES, and refusing means git's ordinary
# conflict markers.
#
# THE UNCONFIGURED CASE IS SAFE, AND THAT IS WHY THIS BINDING IS TRACKED.
# git treats an attribute naming an unconfigured driver as a fallback, not an
# error. Measured on git 2.50.1, fresh repo, this line present and no
# merge.as-built-log.* config at all: `git merge` → ordinary CONFLICT, exit 1,
# and `git apply --3way` succeeds. So a fresh clone, an outside contributor and
# GitHub's own server-side merge get exactly today's behaviour, and a checkout
# that has run scripts/install-merge-drivers.sh gets the clean entry-aware merge.
# (Exit 128, "custom merge driver ... lacks command line", is reachable ONLY from
# a half-written config that sets merge.as-built-log.name without .driver — which
# the installer now cannot produce, because it never writes .name at all.)
docs/AS_BUILT.md merge=as-built-log
